  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 111 PM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nThis Afternoon and Evening\n\nRain-cooled air in the wake of the morning linear mesoscale- \nconvective complex will greatly limit the redevelopment of\nshowers and storms across much of the Upper Mississippi River \nValley through this evening. The latest CAMs are in agreement \nwith this for much of the area. The only areas where they are \nshowing redevelopment late this afternoon and evening is north- \ncentral Wisconsin and far southern Wisconsin. These areas will \nhave a residual convective outflow boundaries from this morning\nand they have a much better chance for recovery.\n\nCurrently have high temperatures ranging from the mid-70s to\nlower 80s. With broken clouds and rain-cooled air from this\nmorning's convection, I am a bit concerned that these \ntemperatures might be a bit too warm.\n\nLow temperatures tonight will range from the mid-50s to around\n60 along and north of Interstate 94, and in the mid-60s\nelsewhere.\n\nMonday \n\nA front will remain primarily south across the Mid-Mississippi \nRiver Valley on Monday. This front will be the focus for \ndevelopment of showers and storms on Monday. In our area, this \nprecipitation would be located across northeast Iowa and \nsouthwest Wisconsin. With the better instability (2-3K J/kg) and\nshear (0-3 km shear around 40 knots) to the south of our area, \nnot anticipating any organized severe weather.\n\nHigh temperatures on Monday will be in the lower and mid-80s. \n\nMonday Night into Tuesday\n\nThe 09.12z models are in good agreement a mesoscale convective\ncomplex will develop across the Dakotas on Monday evening and\nthen move southeast across either the Upper and/or Mid- \nMississippi River Valleys overnight on Monday night and Tuesday \nmorning. With 40-50 knots of uniform winds in the mid-levels, \nthere could be the potential for this system to provide a wind \nthreat. This will be highly dependent on the depth of the \nnocturnal inversion and the amount of DCAPE available.\n\nOn Monday night, low temperatures will range from the mid-50s \nto around 60 along and north of Interstate 94, and in the \nmid-60s elsewhere. High temperatures on Tuesday will be in the\nlower and mid-80s.\n\nTuesday night into Next Weekend\n\nA front will oscillate north and south across the Mid- and \nUpper Mississippi River Valleys from Tuesday night into next\nweekend as shortwave troughs move along the northern periphery \nof a ridge of high pressure over the Southern Plains and \nsoutheast US. The better rain chances will be south of \nInterstate 94. With strong unidirectional flow in the mid- \nlevels, there could be the potential for severe weather at \ntimes. \n\nHigh temperatures will be near normal on Wednesday and generally\nbelow normal from Thursday into Sunday. Low temperatures will\nrange from the mid-50s to mid-60s.",
